  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA cool northwest flow with 850mb temperatures around 0 to +2C will \nkeep the stratus clouds in place over the area today. Forecast \nsoundings from the RRFS and GFS show moisture trapped under an \ninversion (around 860mb) at KBGM and surrounding locations. The \ninversion eventually lowers and weakens heading into the evening and \novernight hours. Temperatures were raised for early this morning, as \nit should hold in the mid-30s to low 40s, and with the clouds locked \nin place it will only rise a few degrees through the day today.\n\nBy tonight the lake/upslope induced stratus clouds should begin to \nscatter out and clear from west to northeast. If the skies truly are \nable to clear out as forecast, then tonight's low temperatures are \nexpected to be cooler, ranging from the mid-20s to lower 30s.\n\nHigh pressure builds in for Tuesday and 850mb temperatures rise to \naround +5 C as the 850mb ridge axis moves east of the area. This \nshould allow for plenty of sunshine and mild temperatures. A large \nupper level ridge then peaks over the area on Wednesday, with 500mb \nheights reaching 582-585dm by 00z Thursday. This is close to 2 \nstandard deviations above normal for mid to late November. 850mb \ntemperatures also continue to rise, reaching +8C. Light west \nsouthwest winds and plenty of sunshine on Wednesday will allow for \ndaytime highs to reach into the 50s and even around 60s in the \nvalleys. The upper level ridge axis moves just east of the area by \nThursday, opening the door to some light southerly winds. Skies are \nagain mainly sunny and high temperatures inch even warmer into the \nmid-50s to lower 60s around the region.\n\nA weak disturbance and surface front press through Thursday night, \nflatting the anomalous 500mb ridge. Surface winds also shift west-\nnorthwest heading into Friday. Skies look to remain mostly sunny and \ntemperatures remain well above average. The next upper level ridge \nbuilds over the region on Saturday, bringing more sunshine and \nwarmth to the area. There remains some uncertainty in the forecast \nfor Sunday, as there will be front approaching from the west and \nalso a possible low off the Southeast coast. Current ensemble \nguidance keeps it dry, partly sunny and warm for the second half of \nthe weekend. However, it should be noted that some of the most \nrecent deterministic guidance does bring the cold front it slightly \nfaster, with shower chances increasing. Timing trends will continue \nto be monitored on this in the coming days.",
